{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"case\": \"K3\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 34 items]\\n[Not shown: 10 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model enabling AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through a revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement, under which NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e13, e18, e22, e25, e29, e31, e35, e61, e82, e91, e115, e150, e204; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e12 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Under the model NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, a usage-based recurring stream. NVIDIA positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model; Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating.\\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. … According to\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e13 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to The Information, as reported July 1, 2026, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of these providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement (sources unnamed).\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23, e26, e30, e36, e62, e83, e92, e122, e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e16 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced on Thursday a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give AI startups access to NVIDIA-powered computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with NVIDIA, which acts as an intermediary. NVIDIA named two initial Australian partners, offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e29 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Nvidia is acting as a financial backstop for neocloud customers purchasing its AI chips, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of the customers' revenue; Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e30 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, and Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [fact; Luke James (Tom's Hardware); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new \\\"revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" under which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (described in the co-authored blog post as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream), while developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. The blog post was co-authored by CFO Colette Kress; neither NVIDIA nor its partners have disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\\n    Quote: In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. … Sharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. … Under the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud service\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement with NVIDIA runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, the Nasdaq-listed neocloud, separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e38 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e82 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia (blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress) 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia on July 1, 2026 unveiled a financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent back idle GPU capacity at a predetermined fixed rate, in exchange for a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on that capacity. Nvidia described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\\" The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed, and Nvidia declined to provide further details beyond the blog post.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e83 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with Nvidia, as an adopter of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e84 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference-architecture specifications, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs, as a second adopter of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] … Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e91 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers under which operators can procure Nvidia compute infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold on that capacity; Nvidia receives its standard product revenue from infrastructure sales plus a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity. The model targets startups, enterprises, researchers and regional providers that lack the balance-sheet strength for capital-intensive builds, and Nvidia said it is tied to future usage rather than only conventional long-term commitments.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers. … Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services s\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e92 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI is among the first companies to collaborate with Nvidia under the new revenue-sharing financing model and plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, as one of the first collaborations with Nvidia under the new revenue-sharing financing model; the campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. … Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e104 [third_party_view; barchart.com (Yahoo Finance); original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Seaport Research analysts published a note (cited as \\\"last week\\\" by the article) arguing that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues and flagging a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\": the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips — directly via equity or debt, or by allowing financing of increasingly expensive chips — the more the demand investors see may not be real to the extent Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, raising earnings-quality concerns. This is the analysts' claim/characterization, not a confirmed figure.\\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. … That said, concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by \\n- e115 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Nvidia has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself, providing neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange sharing in a portion of the neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e116 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Nvidia has also started backstopping datacenter leases, going beyond GPU rental offtake guarantees.\\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e117 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports Nvidia's backstop program is typically six years, during which Nvidia stands ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time, with each neocloud negotiating terms individually; in an illustrative example at the lower end of the range, the backstop averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and SemiAnalysis expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative\\n- e118 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says neither Nvidia nor the neoclouds intend to use the backstop, and if it is triggered the backstop levels would leave a typical neocloud with zero or slightly negative project IRR, but still able to cover debt payments.\\n    Quote: It is important to highlight that while Nvidia does have meaningful internal compute needs, neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop, and thus it is not surprising that the backstop levels would result in a typical Neocloud earning a zero or slightly negative project IRR. … That said, this scenario is exactly why the structure is financeable. Though the IRRs earned \\n- e119 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says banks lending against Nvidia backstops underwrite the debt-service coverage ratio assuming the backstop is activated, require at least 1.3x DSCR, particularly in the first few years, and size loans to a 70-80% loan-to-value ratio.\\n    Quote: When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for Nvidia-backstopped clusters to price wider than current five-year backstopped hyperscale compute deals at around SOFR+225bps, or about 5.9% total yield, but tighter than CoreWeave's five-year unsecured bond at around 10%.\\n    Quote: We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\\n- e121 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's backstop program is only getting started and expects announcements faster every quarter, with many neoclouds already in negotiations with Nvidia and others considering engaging; it has also seen many other neoclouds with backstops that are not public yet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program. … We have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\\n- e122 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia scaling to as many as 40,000 Nvidia GB300 GPUs under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, implying an average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour over six years, and guides to a 132MW footprint with 102MW contracted and more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the project still needs a datacenter provider or self-built datacenter before GPU deployment.\\n    Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. … Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six\\n- e150 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced, on July 1, 2026, a business model letting AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories; participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services, and NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. The revenue-share percentage and how usage is measured were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: The News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of th\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e151 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA / Sharon AI / Firmus 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA named as first partners under the July 1, 2026 financing model: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; Firmus is co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA's DSX blueprints and says the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners. … Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e152 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Futurum Group ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group states that Firmus carries $25–30 billion in committed off-take and lists as unresolved whether that committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Futurum Group ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group notes that NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue-share percentage under its July 1, 2026 revenue-sharing model, nor how usage of the supported capacity is measured.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue share percentage or how usage is measured.\\n- e204 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. (with CFO Colette Kress) 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA disclosed a profit-sharing program giving model builders, AI startups and businesses access to NVIDIA's computing framework without having to fund hardware buildouts; under the arrangement, NVIDIA will receive, in addition to product revenue, a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to end users. In a blog post, NVIDIA Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress described the resulting income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream that removes the need for location selection, building, energy procurement and hardware installation before accessing hyperscale computing. According to the company, the model intensifies engagement compared with ownership stakes in next-generation cloud firms such as Nebius and CoreWeave.\\n    Quote: On July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers de\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Themes in force, 2]\\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's projection of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence implies the same outcome: from about $650 billion in 2026, the projection implies growth of more than 54% to over $1 trillion in 2027. New evidence on Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance, clou\\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the latest reported position: as of July 2, 2026, Chinese authorities had not publicly authorized H200 imports, which is rung 2; no forward consensus, Chinese authorization plan, or deadline commitment is supplied. The evidence implies no public authorization by January 31, 2027: U.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is the market/company expectation for the reported total, but no consensus figure or NVIDIA guidance for it is supplied, so the reference has no assignable rung. The evidence implies about $21.5 billion outstanding: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop; scaling that value by Firmus's planned 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus at full scale. I discount Firmus to roughly $16.6 billion because its project still needs a data-center provider or a self-built facility before GPU deployment, then add Sharon's $4.88 billion. The resulting estimate is just above the $20 billion rung-4 boundary. v1 is not yet judged and supplies no quantitative constraint. The prior rung was 2; the newly quantified Sharon backstop and the comparab\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reference\": \"No consensus figure or NVIDIA guidance for the FY2027 year-end total is supplied, so the reference has no assignable rung.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is not assignable to a ladder rung: no market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for this reported total is supplied. The implied estimate is about $21.5 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop plus an estimated Firmus backstop of about $20.7 billion at full scale (scaling Sharon's value by 170,000 versus 40,000 GPUs), discounted about 20% to $16.6 billion because Firmus still needs a data-center provider or must build its own. This puts the forecast just above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below $40 billion. The announced datacenter-lease backstops and additional negotiations could add exposure, but are not sized, so I do not add them to the estimate. v1 is not yet judged and supplies no quantitative constraint. The updated AI-capex theme supports the higher direction, but does not establish a larger NVIDIA-specific amount.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $21.5 billion outstanding: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $20.7 billion Firmus backstop at full scale, calculated by scaling Sharon's six-year amount by 170,000/40,000 GPUs, then discounted about 20% to $16.6 billion for Firmus's unresolved data-center provision.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"case\": \"K7\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 35 items]\\n[Not shown: 14 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model enabling AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through a revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement, under which NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e13, e18, e22, e25, e29, e31, e35, e61, e82, e91, e115, e150, e204, e251; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e12 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Under the model NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, a usage-based recurring stream. NVIDIA positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model; Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating.\\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. … According to\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e13 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to The Information, as reported July 1, 2026, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of these providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement (sources unnamed).\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23, e26, e30, e36, e62, e83, e92, e122, e151, e252; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e16 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced on Thursday a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give AI startups access to NVIDIA-powered computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with NVIDIA, which acts as an intermediary. NVIDIA named two initial Australian partners, offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e29 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Nvidia is acting as a financial backstop for neocloud customers purchasing its AI chips, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of the customers' revenue; Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e30 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, and Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [fact; Luke James (Tom's Hardware); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new \\\"revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" under which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (described in the co-authored blog post as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream), while developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. The blog post was co-authored by CFO Colette Kress; neither NVIDIA nor its partners have disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\\n    Quote: In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. … Sharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. … Under the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud service\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement with NVIDIA runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, the Nasdaq-listed neocloud, separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e38 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e82 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia (blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress) 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia on July 1, 2026 unveiled a financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent back idle GPU capacity at a predetermined fixed rate, in exchange for a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on that capacity. Nvidia described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\\" The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed, and Nvidia declined to provide further details beyond the blog post.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e84 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference-architecture specifications, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs, as a second adopter of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] … Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e104 [third_party_view; barchart.com (Yahoo Finance); original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Seaport Research analysts published a note (cited as \\\"last week\\\" by the article) arguing that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues and flagging a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\": the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips — directly via equity or debt, or by allowing financing of increasingly expensive chips — the more the demand investors see may not be real to the extent Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, raising earnings-quality concerns. This is the analysts' claim/characterization, not a confirmed figure.\\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. … That said, concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by \\n- e115 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Nvidia has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes itself, providing neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity — and in exchange sharing in a portion of the neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e116 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Nvidia has also started backstopping datacenter leases, going beyond GPU rental offtake guarantees.\\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e117 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports Nvidia's backstop program is typically six years, during which Nvidia stands ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time, with each neocloud negotiating terms individually; in an illustrative example at the lower end of the range, the backstop averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and SemiAnalysis expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative\\n- e118 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says neither Nvidia nor the neoclouds intend to use the backstop, and if it is triggered the backstop levels would leave a typical neocloud with zero or slightly negative project IRR, but still able to cover debt payments.\\n    Quote: It is important to highlight that while Nvidia does have meaningful internal compute needs, neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop, and thus it is not surprising that the backstop levels would result in a typical Neocloud earning a zero or slightly negative project IRR. … That said, this scenario is exactly why the structure is financeable. Though the IRRs earned \\n- e119 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says banks lending against Nvidia backstops underwrite the debt-service coverage ratio assuming the backstop is activated, require at least 1.3x DSCR, particularly in the first few years, and size loans to a 70-80% loan-to-value ratio.\\n    Quote: When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for Nvidia-backstopped clusters to price wider than current five-year backstopped hyperscale compute deals at around SOFR+225bps, or about 5.9% total yield, but tighter than CoreWeave's five-year unsecured bond at around 10%.\\n    Quote: We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\\n- e121 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's backstop program is only getting started and expects announcements faster every quarter, with many neoclouds already in negotiations with Nvidia and others considering engaging; it has also seen many other neoclouds with backstops that are not public yet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program. … We have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\\n- e122 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia scaling to as many as 40,000 Nvidia GB300 GPUs under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, implying an average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour over six years, and guides to a 132MW footprint with 102MW contracted and more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the project still needs a datacenter provider or self-built datacenter before GPU deployment.\\n    Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. … Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six\\n- e150 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced, on July 1, 2026, a business model letting AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories; participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services, and NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. The revenue-share percentage and how usage is measured were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: The News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of th\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e151 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA / Sharon AI / Firmus 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA named as first partners under the July 1, 2026 financing model: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; Firmus is co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA's DSX blueprints and says the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners. … Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e152 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Futurum Group ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group states that Firmus carries $25–30 billion in committed off-take and lists as unresolved whether that committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Futurum Group ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group notes that NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue-share percentage under its July 1, 2026 revenue-sharing model, nor how usage of the supported capacity is measured.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue share percentage or how usage is measured.\\n- e204 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. (with CFO Colette Kress) 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA disclosed a profit-sharing program giving model builders, AI startups and businesses access to NVIDIA's computing framework without having to fund hardware buildouts; under the arrangement, NVIDIA will receive, in addition to product revenue, a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to end users. In a blog post, NVIDIA Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress described the resulting income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream that removes the need for location selection, building, energy procurement and hardware installation before accessing hyperscale computing. According to the company, the model intensifies engagement compared with ownership stakes in next-generation cloud firms such as Nebius and CoreWeave.\\n    Quote: On July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers de\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e245 [fact; AFR Street Talk; original source Nvidia 2026-07-08; from gnews:91a2f447977f812557cf0f6c 2026-07-08] Nvidia has subscribed for about US$500 million (A$721 million) of preference shares in Singapore-based AI data-centre/neocloud operator Firmus Technologies, making it one of the biggest investors in Firmus's US$2 billion (A$2.9 billion) equity raising; the preference shares are designed to convert to ordinary equity at Firmus's ASX initial public offering later this year.\\n    Quote: Street Talk can reveal that the $US4.9 trillion chip developer has put up its hand for about $US500 million ($721 million) worth of Firmus’ preference shares, which are designed to convert to ordinary equity at the initial public offering later this year. … Nasdaq-listed Nvidia has emerged as one of the biggest investors in Firmus Technologies’ $US2 billion ($2.9 billion) equity raising, which is \\n- e251 [fact; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA announced a new revenue-sharing and credit-support model under which it will guarantee the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (neoclouds) over several years and share in revenue earned above the guaranteed/backstop level. In a joint announcement blog, CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri described it as enabling AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure through 'economic alignment,' with AI clouds selling NVIDIA-powered cloud services and NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing 'a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.'\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares tumbled on recent news that the vendor will guarantee the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (aka neoclouds) over several years. In return, NVIDIA will share in revenues earned above the guaranteed, or backstop, level. … “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a re\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e252 [fact; Futuriom, citing NVIDIA; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Under NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, Australia-based AI cloud provider Sharon AI has signed on and will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e253 [fact; Futuriom, citing NVIDIA; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Under NVIDIA's new model, Indonesia-based Firmus has signed on and is building a DSX AI factory there that is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e290 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA estimates Nvidia's total ecosystem investments across companies including OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent and Marvell at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow, concluding the scale looks manageable.\\n    Quote: Strategic investments across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others also raise a fair question: is Nvidia financing its own demand? BofA’s answer is that the scale still looks manageable. It estimates total ecosystem investments at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Themes in force, 2]\\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-10; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: No market-consensus figure is supplied. The operative reference is NVIDIA's projection that combined hyperscaler data-center capex could reach or exceed $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains at least $1 trillion: the approximately $650 billion 2026 figure is a baseline, n\\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the supplied evidence does not establish that Chinese authorities have publicly authorized any company to import H200s, and the latest status evidence describes access as restricted or slow. The forecast is rung 1 because specific reports say officials told Alibaba, ByteDanc\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated target for this reported total is supplied, so there is no reference rung to stay at. The forecast is about $22.0 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, plus an estimated Firmus backstop of about $20.74 billion at full scale ($4.88 billion × 170,000 / 40,000 GPUs), discounted 20% to about $16.59 billion because Firmus still needs a data-center provider or must build its own, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment. The total is above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below its $40 billion ceiling. Firmus's $25–30 billion of expected customer revenue over six years supports the scale of the project but is not itself NVIDIA's backstop amount. The estimate also does not add unquantified l\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated aggregate target for the FY2027 year-end total is supplied; the reference therefore has no rung on this ladder.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"There is no supplied market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for the aggregate outstanding total, so the reference has no assigned rung. The best estimate is about $22 billion: scale Firmus's estimated backstop from Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion for up to 40,000 GPUs to Firmus's target of up to 170,000 GPUs ($20.74 billion), discount that estimate 20% for Firmus's unresolved data-center provider/buildout ($16.59 billion), then add Sharon's $4.88 billion and NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment. Firmus's $25–30 billion of expected customer revenue over six years is not itself NVIDIA's backstop amount. This is above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below its $40 billion ceiling. The newly explicit Firmus backstop supports including the scaled estimate, but its full target capacity is not yet in hand; the dependencies provide no judged revenue constraint, and the updated AI-capex theme supports expansion without quantifying another commitment.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $22 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated Firmus backstop of $4.88 billion × (170,000 / 40,000) = $20.74 billion, discounted 20% to about $16.59 billion for unresolved data-center execution, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"case\": \"K11\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 41 items]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model under which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, aligning the parties through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-pow\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e18, e20, e23, e27, e33, e58, e79, e89, e146, e200; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Firmus Technologies ; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] Firmus Technologies is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14, e15, e22, e25, e28, e36, e60, e81, e91, e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e12 [fact; The Information (relayed by moomoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On 1 July 2026 NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, under which AI cloud providers can procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and NVIDIA earns a share of cloud service revenue in addition to standard product revenue. The Information reported the same day that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud providers that lease its GPUs — if they fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price — in return for a percentage of those providers' revenue that gradually declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program; three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement and an NVIDIA spokesperson confirmed the program's existence.\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e18 [party_statement; Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO, blog post); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d 2026-07-01] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, in a blog post, said the company will grant token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales, and will connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, establishing a new recurring 'usage-linked earnings stream' for NVIDIA. The revenue-sharing model targets researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources; under the branding 'DSX AI factories', an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider like Firmus Technologies faster than going through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales. … The company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \\\"usagelinked earnings stream\\\" for itself, chief financial of\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e20 [fact; qz.com (citing an NVIDIA blog post by Kress); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday that gives AI startups, model builders and enterprises access to NVIDIA computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. Under the arrangement, revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to NVIDIA as AI cloud companies provide NVIDIA-powered services to their end customers; NVIDIA's Kress, in a blog post, called the resulting income a 'recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.' Token credits are also part of the offering.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. … Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end custo\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e21 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. Sharon AI cofounder and CEO James Manning called the collaboration a 'pivotal moment' in the company's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. … \\\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\\\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e29, e59, e80, e90, e147; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Firmus Technologies as the second initial partner in its revenue-sharing program; a campus Firmus is developing in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. Firmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield said AI-native companies need scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. … \\\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\\\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e23 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia announced a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give fast-growing AI startups access to critical computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia. Nvidia named two initial Australian partners; the two are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e24 [fact; CNBC; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, one of the two initial Australian AI-cloud partners Nvidia named for the program, will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [fact; CNBC; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies, an AI infrastructure company and the other initial Australian partner, says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e27 [party_statement; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model lets AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity; under the model NVIDIA acts as a financial backstop and agrees to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. … In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \\\"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e28 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e29 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Sharon AI is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e33 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new business model, pitched in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as a 'revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' under which partner AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream). Per Bloomberg, developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, and neither NVIDIA nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e34 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement under NVIDIA's new model runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions (to Digital Alpha and under the NVIDIA model).\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e36 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n- e58 [fact; mitrade.com (citing NVIDIA's X/Twitter post of 2 July 2026); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new AI compute framework under which cloud providers deploy advanced NVIDIA hardware through revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements, aimed at lowering infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers and regional AI operators; NVIDIA earns revenue both from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by the supported capacity. The framework was announced by NVIDIA in an X/Twitter post dated 2 July 2026 and reported by mitrade.com; no amount of credit support or revenue-sharing terms was disclosed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers. … NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and region\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e59 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support AI compute initiative, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The item gives no timing, contract value or credit-support amount for the deployment.\\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e60 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new AI compute initiative, is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity. No timeline, contract value or credit-support amount is given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled on July 1, 2026 a financing vehicle called the \\\"AI Compute Partnership\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, while taking a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. NVIDIA described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\"; the revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and NVIDIA declined to provide further details.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI, an Australian sovereign-cloud provider and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA; the item also describes it as planning that deployment.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e81 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to NVIDIA's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] … Firmus Techn\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e89 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] NVIDIA launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers under which AI cloud operators can procure NVIDIA infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold on that capacity; NVIDIA receives its standard product revenue from the infrastructure sales plus a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers. … Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services s\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e90 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the model.\\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e91 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e103 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Analysts at Seaport Research published a note (referenced as appearing 'last week' in a barchart.com article dated 5 July 2026) raising concerns about a 'circular-financing boom': they argued that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips — directly via equity or debt, or by allowing financing of these increasingly expensive chips — the more it could mean the demand investors see is not real to the extent Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, and could lead to issues around earnings quality. The same note also flagged that Nvidia's balance sheet has 'growing issues' with 'plenty of turbulence underneath the surface.'\\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. … That said, concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by \\n- e110 [fact; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] The item notes Nvidia is 'fresh off launching' a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, referring to it as a recently completed step with no financial terms, participants, amounts or date disclosed.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\\n- e111 [fact; SemiAnalysis (newsletter.semianalysis.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that Nvidia runs a backstop program in which it gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in return shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; lenders rely on Nvidia's backstop and its AA/Aa2 investment-grade credit rating, lending to match the length of the backstop.\\n    Quote: In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level. … Capital: Lenders look to Nvidia’s backstop and its AA/Aa2 investment grade credit rating and are satisfied to lend matching the length \\n- e112 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis describes Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years long, during which Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time. It presents an illustrative backstop pricing curve on the lower end of the range averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops, and estimates Nvidia's take rate averages about 18% over six years in its short-tenor rental scenario.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. … we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate h\\n- e113 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that Nvidia, beyond backstopping GPU rental offtakes, has begun backstopping datacenter leases.\\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\\n- e114 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over six years). Its footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. … Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the compa\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e115 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus's 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced on June 29, 2026, is a major project announced with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, a portion of which (above the backstop level) SemiAnalysis expects to be shared with Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. … Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six\\n- e146 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced a business model on 1 July 2026 under which AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support: participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services, while NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. According to the analyst, NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue-share percentage or how usage is measured.\\n    Quote: The News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of th\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e147 [fact; Futurum Group; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia as one of the first partners under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e148 [fact; Futurum Group; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus is building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, as one of the first partners under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, and is co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA's DSX blueprints.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners. … Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e149 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group writes that Firmus, one of the first partners building a DSX-aligned AI factory under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, and flags as an open question whether that converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n- e200 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA disclosed a profit-sharing program giving model builders, AI startups and businesses access to NVIDIA's computing framework without needing to fund hardware buildouts; under the arrangement, NVIDIA will receive, in addition to product revenue, a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to their end users. CFO Colette Kress framed the income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream that removes the need for location selection, building, energy procurement and hardware installation to access hyperscale computing. The company said the model intensifies engagement compared with ownership stakes in cloud firms such as Nebius and CoreWeave.\\n    Quote: On July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers de\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Themes in force, 1]\\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains above $1 trillion: that forecast compares with about $650 billion planned for 2026, implying growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The reported $130 billion of blocked or dela\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is not placeable: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The evidence implies about $25.6 billion of nominal backstops: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop, and scaling that same-programme deal by Firmus's 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 gives about $20.74 billion for Firmus. Firmus's capacity is a target rather than fully demonstrated volume, so the estimate is uncertain, but the announced backstop and six-year term support a total above $20 billion; it remains below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The $25–30 billion of Firmus customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the past OpenAI funding participation does not establish an amount still outstanding at FY20\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan supplies a total outstanding commitment amount; the reference is not placeable on the ladder.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"No consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives the total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The best quantified estimate is about $29.3 billion: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop for 72 MW, and Firmus's 360 MW project implies about $24.4 billion when scaled at the same amount per MW; together these are below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The six-year Sharon commitment remains within its term at FY2027 year-end. Firmus's $25–30 billion of customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the additional datacenter-lease backstops are unquantified, so neither is added to the estimate.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $29.3 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $24.4 billion for Firmus, scaling the disclosed Sharon backstop by Firmus's 360 MW versus Sharon's 72 MW. The six-year term and June 2026 announcement mean the Sharon commitment is still within term at the due date; the estimate assumes the scaled Firmus commitment is likewise outstanding.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"case\": \"K15\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 26 items]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model under which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, aligning the parties through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-pow\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e18, e20, e23, e27, e33, e58, e79; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Firmus Technologies ; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] Firmus Technologies is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14, e15, e22, e25, e28, e36, e60, e81; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e12 [fact; The Information (relayed by moomoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On 1 July 2026 NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, under which AI cloud providers can procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and NVIDIA earns a share of cloud service revenue in addition to standard product revenue. The Information reported the same day that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud providers that lease its GPUs — if they fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price — in return for a percentage of those providers' revenue that gradually declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program; three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement and an NVIDIA spokesperson confirmed the program's existence.\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e18 [party_statement; Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO, blog post); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d 2026-07-01] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, in a blog post, said the company will grant token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales, and will connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, establishing a new recurring 'usage-linked earnings stream' for NVIDIA. The revenue-sharing model targets researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources; under the branding 'DSX AI factories', an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider like Firmus Technologies faster than going through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales. … The company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \\\"usagelinked earnings stream\\\" for itself, chief financial of\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e20 [fact; qz.com (citing an NVIDIA blog post by Kress); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday that gives AI startups, model builders and enterprises access to NVIDIA computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. Under the arrangement, revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to NVIDIA as AI cloud companies provide NVIDIA-powered services to their end customers; NVIDIA's Kress, in a blog post, called the resulting income a 'recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.' Token credits are also part of the offering.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. … Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end custo\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e21 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. Sharon AI cofounder and CEO James Manning called the collaboration a 'pivotal moment' in the company's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. … \\\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\\\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e29, e59, e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Firmus Technologies as the second initial partner in its revenue-sharing program; a campus Firmus is developing in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. Firmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield said AI-native companies need scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. … \\\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\\\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e23 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia announced a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give fast-growing AI startups access to critical computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia. Nvidia named two initial Australian partners; the two are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e24 [fact; CNBC; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, one of the two initial Australian AI-cloud partners Nvidia named for the program, will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [fact; CNBC; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies, an AI infrastructure company and the other initial Australian partner, says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e27 [party_statement; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model lets AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity; under the model NVIDIA acts as a financial backstop and agrees to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. … In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \\\"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e28 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e29 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Sharon AI is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e33 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new business model, pitched in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as a 'revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' under which partner AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream). Per Bloomberg, developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, and neither NVIDIA nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e34 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement under NVIDIA's new model runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n- e35 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions (to Digital Alpha and under the NVIDIA model).\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e36 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n- e58 [fact; mitrade.com (citing NVIDIA's X/Twitter post of 2 July 2026); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new AI compute framework under which cloud providers deploy advanced NVIDIA hardware through revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements, aimed at lowering infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers and regional AI operators; NVIDIA earns revenue both from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by the supported capacity. The framework was announced by NVIDIA in an X/Twitter post dated 2 July 2026 and reported by mitrade.com; no amount of credit support or revenue-sharing terms was disclosed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers. … NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and region\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e59 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support AI compute initiative, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The item gives no timing, contract value or credit-support amount for the deployment.\\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e60 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new AI compute initiative, is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity. No timeline, contract value or credit-support amount is given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled on July 1, 2026 a financing vehicle called the \\\"AI Compute Partnership\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, while taking a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. NVIDIA described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\"; the revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and NVIDIA declined to provide further details.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI, an Australian sovereign-cloud provider and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA; the item also describes it as planning that deployment.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e81 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to NVIDIA's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] … Firmus Techn\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference is supplied, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. My base-case forecast remains below $5 billion: the evidence now includes a six-year Sharon AI agreement covering up to 40,000 GPUs and reports that Firmus adopted the backstop, but the disclosed deployments are capacity plans, not evidence that NVIDIA guarantees the full value of all that capacity. The backstop applies to unused capacity at a pre-agreed price, and the amount, price, and expected unsold share are not disclosed. I therefore forecast exposure from the initial, contingent cohort below the rung-2 threshold, with low confidence; the evidence does not establish that the amount is zero.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the total is supplied; the reference is therefore unplaceable on the ladder.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the reported total is supplied, so the market reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The new program and its initial deployments increase the potential exposure, but the disclosed GPU and MW capacities are not dollar commitments, and NVIDIA's backstop amount, pricing, and expected unused capacity remain unspecified. I forecast the outstanding total below $5 billion rather than counting the full value of the planned deployments. The $30 billion OpenAI participation is a significant financing fact, but it is described as cash already funnelled into a funding round, not as an outstanding commitment at FY2027 year-end; the separate $200 million Digital Alpha facility is not an NVIDIA commitment. The evidence therefore does not support moving across the $5 billion boundary, though the forecast remains uncertain.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast: below $5 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). The announced backstop has no disclosed dollar cap or utilization assumptions, so the announced GPU capacity cannot be converted into an outstanding commitment; the $30 billion OpenAI participation is described as funding already provided, not an outstanding year-end commitment.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"case\": \"K19\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \\\"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 27 items]\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 247wallst.com; original source 247wallst.com (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The article estimates that about 50% of NVIDIA's Q1 FY27 data center revenue comes from hyperscalers—the same companies funding Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n    Quote: About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n- e163 [fact; 247wallst.com (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source Amazon ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] Amazon has disclosed that Trainium, its custom AI chip, is now a multi-billion-dollar business; the article adds that every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA's data center segment also funds an alternative.\\n    Quote: Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative.\\n- e65 [party_statement; Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander, The Diplomat; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA ; from gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang estimated that of every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on NVIDIA's GPUs, according to The Diplomat article citing his remarks.\\n    Quote: For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\\n- e152 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom AI ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators, as Broadcom's custom chips for Alphabet and Meta gain ground against NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e237; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [fact; Danielle Liverance, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Broadcom reported Q2 AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion, up 143% year over year, and guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion.\\n    Quote: Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e237 [third_party_view; Danielle Liverance, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e152; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e249 [fact; idnfinancials.com; original source OpenAI (with Broadcom) ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, in the month before the item's 2026-07-07 publication, in collaboration with Broadcom; the item frames this as part of a broader trend of major AI companies developing their own chips to reduce dependence on Nvidia, and says Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\\n    Quote: Globally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\\n- e305 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA's report says NVIDIA's hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, which BofA reads as wallet-share gains for NVIDIA rather than share loss to custom chips.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e292; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e306 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA's report still models NVIDIA holding roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capex over time, with the remainder split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e311 [fact; Parasail and d-Matrix, via PRNewswire release; original source Parasail and d-Matrix (joint announcement) 2026-07-08; from gnews:040c256543fc8106ffecabae 2026-07-08] Parasail, an inference cloud for AI-native startups, and d-Matrix announced on 2026-07-08 that Parasail is deploying d-Matrix Corsair inference accelerators alongside its existing NVIDIA Hopper and NVIDIA Blackwell GPU fleets to serve inference workloads, combining NVIDIA GPUs for compute-intensive prefill with Corsair for latency-sensitive decode. The companies describe it as one of the first commercial-scale examples of heterogeneous disaggregated inference in production and claim up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference services, with detailed performance results and case studies to follow the first series of deployments.\\n    Quote: Parasail, the inference cloud for AI-native startups, and d-Matrix, a pioneer in low-latency AI inference compute platforms for data centers, today announced Parasail is deploying d-Matrix Corsair inference accelerators alongside the NVIDIA Hopper and NVIDIA Blackwell architectures to deliver up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference services to its customers. … Parasail's Corsair deployment\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e265 (); both are in force: NVIDIA is combining its GPU hardware with inference chips from startup d-Matrix to ship a joint system for running AI models, with AI-cloud firm Parasail as its first customer and the system due online later this year, as reported by The Information.\\n- e312 [fact; PRNewswire release (d-Matrix announcement); original source d-Matrix ; from gnews:040c256543fc8106ffecabae 2026-07-08] d-Matrix, a merchant AI inference compute vendor whose Corsair accelerators Parasail is deploying alongside NVIDIA GPUs, announced (as reported in this item dated 2026-07-08) d-Matrix Squadron, described as a fleet of multi-rack systems offering megawatts of highly efficient inference compute for running AI workloads. The item gives no customer, price, capacity figure beyond 'megawatts', or delivery date for Squadron.\\n    Quote: d-Matrix today announced d-Matrix Squadron, a fleet of multi-rack systems offering megawatts of highly efficient inference compute for running AI...\\n- e316 [third_party_view; Russell Brandom, TechCrunch; original source Ornn (compute-market data) ; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The spot price for an hour of time on an NVIDIA H100 GPU, tracked via the compute-market platform Ornn, peaked around $3.20 an hour in May and then declined steadily over the past year, according to TechCrunch—mirroring the drop in NVIDIA's stock price.\\n    Quote: In comparison, this (via the compute market Ornn ) is how the spot price for an hour of time on an Nvidia H100 GPU has changed over the last year: … Just like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off.\\n- e317 [third_party_view; Russell Brandom, TechCrunch (citing Wayne Nelms, Ornn); original source Google, Amazon, Microsoft and OpenAI ; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] Google, Amazon, Microsoft and OpenAI have launched their own custom AI processors to lessen their dependence on NVIDIA, and although those chips are not as good as NVIDIA's latest models they are good enough to drive down the price of compute, TechCrunch reported, drawing on compute-market executive Wayne Nelms.\\n    Quote: Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n- e320 [third_party_view; Vivek Arya, Bank of America; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, listed the concerns driving Nvidia's sell-off: gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks or dividends.\\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\\n- e338 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projects NVIDIA will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030, a forward estimate of its competitive dominance as other companies build custom chips.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\\n- e339 [fact; qz.com; original source Google ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Google, seven generations into its custom AI chip program, announced its newest inference chip Ironwood earlier in 2026; it can link up to 9,216 Ironwood chips into a single cluster, and any Google Cloud customer in North America and Europe can already rent access to it.\\n    Quote: Google is seven generations deep into its custom AI chip program. The company announced its newest version, called Ironwood, earlier this year. Ironwood is built for inference — the work of running an already trained AI model — and Google can already link up to 9,216 Ironwood chips into a single cluster to run that work at scale. Any Google Cloud customer in North America and Europe can already re\\n- e340 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon (Annapurna Labs) ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon's Annapurna Labs subsidiary said Trainium3, the newest in its fourth generation of AI training chips, went on sale in December 2025; it is AWS's first chip on a more advanced manufacturing process and supports PyTorch, allowing developers to switch to Amazon's chips without rewriting code.\\n    Quote: Amazon is close behind Google . Its Annapurna Labs subsidiary announced that Trainium3, the newest in its fourth generation of AI training chips, went on sale in December 2025. It's also AWS's first chip built on a more advanced manufacturing process than any of its predecessors, roughly the same scale used in today's newest phone chips. The chip also supports PyTorch, the software most developers\\n- e341 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in late 2025 that the prior-generation Trainium2 had no spare capacity left to sell and had grown into a multibillion-dollar business growing at a 150% quarterly clip.\\n    Quote: Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in late 2025 that Trainium2, the previous generation, had no spare capacity left to sell. He said it had grown into a multibillion-dollar business at a 150% quarterly clip.\\n- e342 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon's next custom chip, Trainium4, which packs three times the performance of its predecessor, could arrive later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Amazon 's next chip, Trainium4, which packs three times the performance of its predecessor, could arrive later this year.\\n- e343 [fact; qz.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Microsoft announced its inference chip Maia 200 in January 2026 and the chip is already reaching a handful of U.S. data centers; Microsoft says it runs at 30% better performance per dollar than the fastest hardware in its fleet, and it will power OpenAI's newest models plus Microsoft products like Copilot, connected via standard Ethernet in clusters of up to 6,144 chips.\\n    Quote: Microsoft is moving just as fast, and like Google , it's building for inference rather than training. It announced Maia 200, a chip built for that work, in January 2026, and the chip is already reaching a handful of U.S. data centers. Microsoft says Maia 200 runs at 30% better performance per dollar than the fastest hardware already in its fleet. The chip will power OpenAI's newest models, along w\\n- e344 [fact; qz.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 300 chip is already in production for ranking and recommendation work; Meta plans three more generations over the next two years for generative AI models, with MTIA 450 arriving in early 2027 and doubling memory bandwidth, and successor MTIA 500 expected later in 2027 with a further 50% higher memory speed.\\n    Quote: The newest version, MTIA 300, is already in production for ranking and recommendation work. Meta is set to build three more generations over the next two years with the purpose of running generative AI models. The next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory. Its eventual successor, MTIA 500, is expected to come later next year, and pushes m\\n- e345 [fact; qz.com; original source OpenAI / Broadcom ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] OpenAI announced a partnership with Broadcom in October 2025 to build 10 gigawatts of custom AI chips; none has shipped yet, installation is set to begin in the second half of 2026 and run through the end of 2029, and OpenAI will keep every chip for itself rather than selling cloud access.\\n    Quote: It announced a partnership with Broadcom in October 2025 to build 10 gigawatts of custom AI chips, a measure of how much power the new chips will draw once installed. None of those chips has shipped yet. Installing them is set to begin in the second half of 2026 and run through the end of 2029. Unlike Google , which sells access to its chips through the cloud, OpenAI will keep every one of its chi\\n- e349 [fact; qz.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Broadcom, the chip design and networking partner behind several custom AI chip programs, reported $5.2 billion in AI-related revenue for the third quarter of its 2025 fiscal year.\\n    Quote: The company reported $5.2 billion in AI-related revenue for the third quarter of its 2025 fiscal year.\\n- e353 [rumour; Reuters (relayed by moomoo.com); original source Meta Platforms 2026-07-09; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta Platforms is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its own AI chip in September, working with Broadcom to design it and TSMC to manufacture it, according to a company internal memo cited by Reuters. Meta is NVIDIA's fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, Super Micro Computer and Alphabet (Bloomberg data).\\n    Quote: $Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e368; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e362 [third_party_view; Bloomberg / Jeran Wittenstein (latimes.com); original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] NVIDIA held 97% of the server GPU market at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024, according to data compiled by Bloomberg Intelligence; the item says NVIDIA's market share has hardly been dented even as its largest customers such as Alphabet and Amazon increasingly deploy their own custom-made chips.\\n    Quote: However, the company’s market share has hardly been dented as demand for equipment going into new data centers remains strong. Nvidia had 97% of the server GPU market at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024, according to data compiled by Bloomberg Intelligence.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e368 [fact; Reuters; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing; the report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and AMD.\\n    Quote: Reuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom (AVGO) and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSM). The report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e374 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Major NVIDIA clients including Meta (reported action) ; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus reported that, alongside a 4% rise in NVIDIA shares to $210.96 on 2026-07-10, there were rumors that major NVIDIA clients, including Meta, are accelerating development of their own in-house AI chips — a shift that could reduce NVIDIA's share of Meta's AI-chip spending. The article added that analysts it cited remain optimistic, suggesting that although NVIDIA's share within Meta may decline, overall AI capital expenditures from major cloud service providers are expected to rise significantly. The claim is reported as rumor/concern; no client spend figure, contract, or dated milestone is given.\\n    Quote: On July 10, 2026, NVIDIA's NVDA stock experienced a notable increase, rising 4% to $210.96, despite rumors of major clients accelerating their development of in-house artificial intelligence (AI) chips. … NVIDIA's stock surge comes amidst growing concerns that major clients, such as Meta, are shifting towards developing their own AI chips. This strategic pivot could potentially impact NVIDIA's mar\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is not numerically specified: no supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated plan gives total cloud-provider custom-ASIC spending for calendar 2026, so it cannot be placed on the ladder. The evidence now points to a total around $60–70 billion, rather than the previous estimate of roughly $50 billion: Broadcom’s $16 billion Q3 AI-semiconductor revenue guidance and reported 143% year-over-year growth indicate a rapidly rising supply-side run-rate, while Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta have chips in service or production and OpenAI’s installation program is due to start in 2026. I discount Broadcom’s broader AI-semiconductor revenue because it is not all custom ASICs, and discount OpenAI’s multi-year target because no chips have shipped. The resulting estimate is just a\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reference\": \"No numerical market consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for total calendar-2026 cloud-provider custom-ASIC spending is supplied; the reference therefore has no assignable rung.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"There is no supplied numerical consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for total calendar-2026 cloud-provider custom-ASIC spending, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The latest Broadcom evidence points to a rapidly rising supply run-rate: AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year, with $16.0 billion guided for Q3. That revenue includes more than custom ASICs, so it is not a direct total-spending figure; balanced against it are expanding or capacity-constrained deployments at Amazon and Google, and smaller or later-stage deployments at Microsoft, Meta, and OpenAI. These imply roughly $65–75 billion of 2026 spending, within rung 3 and not enough to cross the $80 billion boundary. The v6 dependency is not yet judged and supplies no input.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $65–75 billion in calendar 2026. Broadcom's $16.0 billion Q3 AI-semiconductor guidance indicates a high and rising run-rate, but includes non-ASIC revenue; allowing for that mix and combining it with the disclosed custom-chip activity across providers supports an estimate in the $60–80 billion band.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"case\": \"K23\",\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 23 items]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model enabling AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through a revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement, under which NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e13, e18, e22, e25, e29, e31, e35, e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e12 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Under the model NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, a usage-based recurring stream. NVIDIA positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model; Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating.\\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. … According to\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e13 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to The Information, as reported July 1, 2026, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return NVIDIA receives a percentage of these providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement (sources unnamed).\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23, e26, e30, e36, e62; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e16 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n- e18 [party_statement; Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO), via blog post; reported by sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d 2026-07-01] NVIDIA Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress, in a blog post published Wednesday (July 1, 2026), said the company is looking to expand access to its AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales. Kress said NVIDIA will help connect AI data-centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \\\"usage-linked earnings stream\\\" for NVIDIA. Under its \\\"DSX AI factories\\\" branding, an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider such as Firmus Technologies with greater speed and flexibility than otherwise.\\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales. … The company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \\\"usagelinked earnings stream\\\" for itself, chief financial of\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced (reported as made \\\"on Wednesday\\\") a revenue-sharing program designed to give AI startups, model builders and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without having to finance hardware buildouts themselves. Under the arrangement, revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to NVIDIA as AI cloud companies provide NVIDIA-powered services to their end customers; NVIDIA's Kress, in a blog post, called the resulting income a \\\"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\\\" and said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up. Token credits are also part of the offering, giving startups access to compute without upfront capital outlays.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. … Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end custo\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e23 [fact; qz.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named two initial partners for the revenue-sharing program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e24 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies, an initial partner in NVIDIA's revenue-sharing program, is developing a campus in Batam, Indonesia planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced on Thursday a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give AI startups access to NVIDIA-powered computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with NVIDIA, which acts as an intermediary. NVIDIA named two initial Australian partners, offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e26 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under NVIDIA's new partnership program, Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e27 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs, as part of NVIDIA's new partnership program.\\n    Quote: AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e29 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Nvidia is acting as a financial backstop for neocloud customers purchasing its AI chips, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of the customers' revenue; Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e30 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, and Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e31 [party_statement; NVIDIA, reported by DatacenterDynamics; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said the model lets AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model; AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services while Nvidia earns standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity, providing Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \\\"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … \\\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [fact; Luke James (Tom's Hardware); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new \\\"revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" under which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (described in the co-authored blog post as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream), while developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. The blog post was co-authored by CFO Colette Kress; neither NVIDIA nor its partners have disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\\n    Quote: In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. … Sharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. … Under the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud service\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement with NVIDIA runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, the Nasdaq-listed neocloud, separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e38 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [fact; mitrade.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new AI compute framework under which cloud providers deploy advanced NVIDIA hardware through revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements, with NVIDIA earning revenue both from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by the supported capacity. NVIDIA said the goal is to lower infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers and regional AI operators and to speed construction of large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories for inference and token-intensive workloads.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and regional AI operators. … The company earns revenue from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by supported capacity. This approach aims to accelerate the const\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e62 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Under NVIDIA's revenue-sharing and credit-support framework, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, cited by the article as an early participant illustrating the scale of the initiative.\\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e63 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus, cited as another early participant in NVIDIA's revenue-sharing and credit-support framework, is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Themes in force, 1]\\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-02; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 2: as of July 2, 2026, Chinese authorities had not publicly authorized H200 imports. No explicit forward market consensus or Chinese authorization plan is supplied, so that current status is the available reference, not proof of the outcome by the deadline. The U.S. has cleared\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No quantified consensus or NVIDIA guidance is supplied, so there is no evidence-based reference rung to compare against. The evidence points to a newly announced, real credit-support model and potential backstops, but it does not state NVIDIA’s dollar exposure; the large partner capacity figures are plans, not amounts of outstanding commitments. Given the short interval to FY2027 year-end and the uncertainty over how much capacity will be deployed and supported by then, the most likely reported total remains below $5 billion, without treating lack of confirmation as proof that no commitments exist.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for this reported total is supplied, so there is no stated market-reference rung. The previous working forecast was below $5 billion (rung 1), but that is only a comparison point, not a reason by itself to remain there.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The new disclosures make the backstop model and participating capacity more concrete, but they do not quantify NVIDIA's outstanding dollar commitments. The 210,000-GPU figure is potential partner capacity, not NVIDIA's financing exposure; the disclosed Sharon AI agreement is for up to 40,000 GPUs over six years, while the Firmus campus is still described as planned capacity. With about eight months to the due date and no disclosed dollar amount or usable price-per-unit figure to convert these plans into NVIDIA exposure, the most likely reported total remains below the $5 billion rung boundary. The possible $500 billion Ohio project is a project-cost estimate amid reported negotiations, not a stated NVIDIA commitment.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Most likely below $5 billion at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). This is a qualitative forecast: the disclosed partner GPU capacities and backstop structure imply exposure, but the evidence supplies neither a commitment amount nor a price basis to translate planned capacity into NVIDIA's outstanding dollar total.\"\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"CASE\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"CASE\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"CASE K3 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 4, \"reference\": \"No consensus figure or NVIDIA guidance for the FY2027 year-end total is supplied, so the reference has no assignable rung.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is not assignable to a ladder rung: no market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for this reported total is supplied. The implied estimate is about $21.5 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop plus an estimated Firmus backstop of about $20.7 billion at full scale (scaling Sharon's value by 170,000 versus 40,000 GPUs), discounted about 20% to $16.6 billion because Firmus still needs a data-center provider or must build its own. This puts the forecast just above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below $40 billion. The announced datacenter-lease backstops and additional negotiations could add exposure, but are not sized, so I do not add them to the estimate. v1 is not yet judged and supplies no quantitative constraint. The updated AI-capex theme supports the higher direction, but does not establish a larger NVIDIA-specific amount.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $21.5 billion outstanding: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $20.7 billion Firmus backstop at full scale, calculated by scaling Sharon's six-year amount by 170,000/40,000 GPUs, then discounted about 20% to $16.6 billion for Firmus's unresolved data-center provision.\"}\nCASE K7 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 4, \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated aggregate target for the FY2027 year-end total is supplied; the reference therefore has no rung on this ladder.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no supplied market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for the aggregate outstanding total, so the reference has no assigned rung. The best estimate is about $22 billion: scale Firmus's estimated backstop from Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion for up to 40,000 GPUs to Firmus's target of up to 170,000 GPUs ($20.74 billion), discount that estimate 20% for Firmus's unresolved data-center provider/buildout ($16.59 billion), then add Sharon's $4.88 billion and NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment. Firmus's $25–30 billion of expected customer revenue over six years is not itself NVIDIA's backstop amount. This is above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below its $40 billion ceiling. The newly explicit Firmus backstop supports including the scaled estimate, but its full target capacity is not yet in hand; the dependencies provide no judged revenue constraint, and the updated AI-capex theme supports expansion without quantifying another commitment.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $22 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated Firmus backstop of $4.88 billion × (170,000 / 40,000) = $20.74 billion, discounted 20% to about $16.59 billion for unresolved data-center execution, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment.\"}\nCASE K11 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 4, \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan supplies a total outstanding commitment amount; the reference is not placeable on the ladder.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives the total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The best quantified estimate is about $29.3 billion: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop for 72 MW, and Firmus's 360 MW project implies about $24.4 billion when scaled at the same amount per MW; together these are below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The six-year Sharon commitment remains within its term at FY2027 year-end. Firmus's $25–30 billion of customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the additional datacenter-lease backstops are unquantified, so neither is added to the estimate.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $29.3 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $24.4 billion for Firmus, scaling the disclosed Sharon backstop by Firmus's 360 MW versus Sharon's 72 MW. The six-year term and June 2026 announcement mean the Sharon commitment is still within term at the due date; the estimate assumes the scaled Firmus commitment is likewise outstanding.\"}\nCASE K15 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the total is supplied; the reference is therefore unplaceable on the ladder.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the reported total is supplied, so the market reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The new program and its initial deployments increase the potential exposure, but the disclosed GPU and MW capacities are not dollar commitments, and NVIDIA's backstop amount, pricing, and expected unused capacity remain unspecified. I forecast the outstanding total below $5 billion rather than counting the full value of the planned deployments. The $30 billion OpenAI participation is a significant financing fact, but it is described as cash already funnelled into a funding round, not as an outstanding commitment at FY2027 year-end; the separate $200 million Digital Alpha facility is not an NVIDIA commitment. The evidence therefore does not support moving across the $5 billion boundary, though the forecast remains uncertain.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast: below $5 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). The announced backstop has no disclosed dollar cap or utilization assumptions, so the announced GPU capacity cannot be converted into an outstanding commitment; the $30 billion OpenAI participation is described as funding already provided, not an outstanding year-end commitment.\"}\nCASE K19 \n Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 3, \"reference\": \"No numerical market consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for total calendar-2026 cloud-provider custom-ASIC spending is supplied; the reference therefore has no assignable rung.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no supplied numerical consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for total calendar-2026 cloud-provider custom-ASIC spending, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The latest Broadcom evidence points to a rapidly rising supply run-rate: AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year, with $16.0 billion guided for Q3. That revenue includes more than custom ASICs, so it is not a direct total-spending figure; balanced against it are expanding or capacity-constrained deployments at Amazon and Google, and smaller or later-stage deployments at Microsoft, Meta, and OpenAI. These imply roughly $65–75 billion of 2026 spending, within rung 3 and not enough to cross the $80 billion boundary. The v6 dependency is not yet judged and supplies no input.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $65–75 billion in calendar 2026. Broadcom's $16.0 billion Q3 AI-semiconductor guidance indicates a high and rising run-rate, but includes non-ASIC revenue; allowing for that mix and combining it with the disclosed custom-chip activity across providers supports an estimate in the $60–80 billion band.\"}\nCASE K23 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"reference\": \"No market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for this reported total is supplied, so there is no stated market-reference rung. The previous working forecast was below $5 billion (rung 1), but that is only a comparison point, not a reason by itself to remain there.\", \"reasons\": \"The new disclosures make the backstop model and participating capacity more concrete, but they do not quantify NVIDIA's outstanding dollar commitments. The 210,000-GPU figure is potential partner capacity, not NVIDIA's financing exposure; the disclosed Sharon AI agreement is for up to 40,000 GPUs over six years, while the Firmus campus is still described as planned capacity. With about eight months to the due date and no disclosed dollar amount or usable price-per-unit figure to convert these plans into NVIDIA exposure, the most likely reported total remains below the $5 billion rung boundary. The possible $500 billion Ohio project is a project-cost estimate amid reported negotiations, not a stated NVIDIA commitment.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Most likely below $5 billion at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). This is a qualitative forecast: the disclosed partner GPU capacities and backstop structure imply exposure, but the evidence supplies neither a commitment amount nor a price basis to translate planned capacity into NVIDIA's outstanding dollar total.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"])\n for s in x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n- \\\"):\n  if re.search(r\\\"4\\\\.88|16\\\\.0 billion|10\\\\.8 billion|preference.share|\\\\\"'$30 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$500 million\",s,re.I): print(s[:1200].split(\"'\"\\\\n    Quote:\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"])\n for s in x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n- \\\"):\n  if re.search(r\\\"4\\\\.88|16\\\\.0 billion|10\\\\.8 billion|preference.share|\\\\\"'$30 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$500 million\",s,re.I): print(s[:1200].split(\"'\"\\\\n    Quote:\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n K3\ne122 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia scaling to as many as 40,000 Nvidia GB300 GPUs under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, implying an average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour over six years, and guides to a 132MW footprint with 102MW contracted and more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\ne123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the project still needs a datacenter provider or self-built datacenter before GPU deployment.\nv8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the latest reported position: as of July 2, 2026, Chinese authorities had not publicly authorized H200 imports, which is rung 2; no forward consensus, Chinese authorization plan, or deadline commitment is supplied. The evidence implies no public authorization by January 31, 2027: U.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is the market/company expectation for the reported total, but no consensus figure or NVIDIA guidance for it is supplied, so the reference has no assignable rung. The evidence implies about $21.5 billion outstanding: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop; scaling that value by Firmus's planned 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus at full scale. I discount Firmus to roughly $16.6 billion because its project still needs a data-center provider or a self-built facility before GPU deployment, then add Sharon's $4.88 billi\n\n K7\ne122 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia scaling to as many as 40,000 Nvidia GB300 GPUs under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, implying an average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour over six years, and guides to a 132MW footprint with 102MW contracted and more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\ne123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the project still needs a datacenter provider or self-built datacenter before GPU deployment.\ne245 [fact; AFR Street Talk; original source Nvidia 2026-07-08; from gnews:91a2f447977f812557cf0f6c 2026-07-08] Nvidia has subscribed for about US$500 million (A$721 million) of preference shares in Singapore-based AI data-centre/neocloud operator Firmus Technologies, making it one of the biggest investors in Firmus's US$2 billion (A$2.9 billion) equity raising; the preference shares are designed to convert to ordinary equity at Firmus's ASX initial public offering later this year.\nv8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the supplied evidence does not establish that Chinese authorities have publicly authorized any company to import H200s, and the latest status evidence describes access as restricted or slow. The forecast is rung 1 because specific reports say officials told Alibaba, ByteDanc\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated target for this reported total is supplied, so there is no reference rung to stay at. The forecast is about $22.0 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, plus an estimated Firmus backstop of about $20.74 billion at full scale ($4.88 billion × 170,000 / 40,000 GPUs), discounted 20% to about $16.59 billion because Firmus still needs a data-center provider or must build its own, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share investment. The total is above rung 4's $20 billion floor and below its $40 billion ceiling. Firmus's $25–30 billio\n\n K11\ne37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\ne114 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over six years). Its footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\nv13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains above $1 trillion: that forecast compares with about $650 billion planned for 2026, implying growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The reported $130 billion of blocked or dela\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is not placeable: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The evidence implies about $25.6 billion of nominal backstops: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop, and scaling that same-programme deal by Firmus's 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 gives about $20.74 billion for Firmus. Firmus's capacity is a target rather than fully demonstrated volume, so the estimate is uncertain, but the announced backstop and six-year term support a total above $20 billion; it remains below the $40 bill\n\n K15\ne37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\n\n K19\ne236 [fact; Danielle Liverance, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Broadcom reported Q2 AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion, up 143% year over year, and guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion.\n\n K23\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"case\\\"] in [\\\"K3\\\",\\\"K7\\\",\\\"K11\\\",\\\"K15\\\",\\\"K19\\\",\\\"K23\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"])\n  for s in x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n- \\\"):\n   if re.match(r\\\"e\\\\d+ \\\\[\\\",s) and re.search(r\\\"170,000|360MW|360 megawatts|200 million|\\\\\"'$500 billion\",s,re.I): print(s.split(\"'\"\\\\n    Quote:\\\")[0][:420])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/self-report/packet-source-check-3.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"case\\\"] in [\\\"K3\\\",\\\"K7\\\",\\\"K11\\\",\\\"K15\\\",\\\"K19\\\",\\\"K23\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"])\n  for s in x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n- \\\"):\n   if re.match(r\\\"e\\\\d+ \\\\[\\\",s) and re.search(r\\\"170,000|360MW|360 megawatts|200 million|\\\\\"'$500 billion\",s,re.I): print(s.split(\"'\"\\\\n    Quote:\\\")[0][:420])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n K3\ne14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\ne15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\ne16 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\ne30 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, and Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\ne37 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, the Nasdaq-listed neocloud, separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\ne38 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\ne84 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference-architecture specifications, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs, as a second adopter of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership.\ne93 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, as one of the first collaborations with Nvidia under the new revenue-sharing financing model; the campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\ne123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the pro\ne151 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA / Sharon AI / Firmus 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA named as first partners under the July 1, 2026 financing model: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; Firmus\n\n K7\ne14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\ne15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\ne16 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\ne30 [fact; DatacenterDynamics; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, and Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\ne37 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, the Nasdaq-listed neocloud, separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\ne38 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\ne84 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference-architecture specifications, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs, as a second adopter of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership.\ne123 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility, with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years, a portion of revenue above the backstop level expected to be shared with Nvidia, and the pro\ne151 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA / Sharon AI / Firmus 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA named as first partners under the July 1, 2026 financing model: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; Firmus\ne253 [fact; Futuriom, citing NVIDIA; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Under NVIDIA's new model, Indonesia-based Firmus has signed on and is building a DSX AI factory there that is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n\n K11\ne2 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Firmus Technologies ; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] Firmus Technologies is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\ne14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\ne15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source F